# dpkg — real-world examples

## Install or upgrade from a .deb file

```sh
sudo dpkg -i test.deb

# then fix any missing dependencies
sudo apt-get install -f
```

## Remove a package including its configuration files (purge)

```sh
sudo dpkg -P package_name
```

## List all installed packages with versions

```sh
dpkg -l
dpkg -l | grep nginx
```

## Is this package installed?

```sh
dpkg -s package_name | grep Status
```

## What files did a package install?

```sh
dpkg -L package_name
```

## Which package owns this file?

```sh
dpkg -S /usr/bin/ssh
```

## Inspect a .deb before installing

```sh
dpkg -I test.deb     # metadata
dpkg -c test.deb     # contents
```

## Note for the OpenSUSE homelab

Tumbleweed is RPM-based — the equivalent workflow is `sudo zypper
install ./pkg.rpm` and `rpm -qa`, `rpm -ql`, `rpm -qf` for queries.
dpkg applies to the Debian/Ubuntu boxes and containers.

| Flag | What it does                     |
| ---- | -------------------------------- |
| -i   | install a .deb                   |
| -P   | purge (remove + config files)    |
| -l   | list installed packages          |
| -s   | status/details of a package      |
| -L   | files installed by a package     |
| -S   | find owner package of a file     |
